## /goal status

`MERGE_READY / NOT_DEPLOYED / LIVE_PROVIDER_UNOBSERVED`

This PR creates the first intentionally narrow paid-hosted commercial path without building a full auth/wallet/Stripe-webhook control plane.

Canonical records:
- `docs/HOSTED_ENTITLEMENT_V0.md`
- `docs/DA_COUNTER_DA_HOSTED_ENTITLEMENT_V0.md`

## Supported first commercial shape

```text
BUY_ONCE or SUBSCRIPTION
+ LEVEL_4_HOSTED_ONLY
+ BYOK only
+ creator-owned Stripe Payment Link
+ operator manually verifies payment
+ one high-entropy bearer entitlement per payment
```

Anything outside that shape fails closed.

## Included

- persistent SQLite entitlement store;
- only SHA-256 bearer-token digests stored at rest;
- payment-bound entitlement metadata and revocation;
- duplicate active entitlement for the same package/payment blocked;
- explicit operator package activation;
- explicit `--payment-verified` required before token issuance;
- BUY_ONCE non-expiring semantics;
- SUBSCRIPTION requires bounded expiry;
- revoke by plaintext bearer token or package/payment reference;
- paid config/chat require `X-WebAI-Entitlement`;
- direct chat bypass blocks before provider execution;
- paid hosted v0 refuses PLATFORM_CREDIT/shared subsidy;
- paid hosted v0 supports BUY_ONCE / SUBSCRIPTION only;
- fragment handoff `#access=...` so bearer is not placed in the initial HTTP request URL;
- buyer UI moves fragment token to sessionStorage and removes it with `window.history.replaceState`;
- no root mount of the unentitled core app on commercial entrypoint;
- HTTPS fail-closed for paid buyer page/config and all commercial chat unless explicitly overridden for local development;
- paid buyer page `no-store`, `no-referrer`, frame denial, restrictive permissions and CSP;
- commercial Studio readiness adapter for the narrow manual entitlement path without silent activation;
- systemd commercial deployment template;
- minimal Caddy HTTPS reverse-proxy template.

## DA / Counter-DA findings fixed

1. Shared paid URL was not buyer entitlement.
2. Plaintext entitlement storage would amplify a DB leak.
3. Lost plaintext token made revocation operationally weak.
4. Duplicate manual issue could create multiple active tokens per payment.
5. Subscription could accidentally become permanent.
6. Operator could issue access without explicitly affirming payment verification.
7. Shared PLATFORM_CREDIT could recreate uncontrolled paid-user subsidy.
8. Root-mounted core app left a future routing-order authority bypass surface.
9. Paid UI shadowed the browser History API, breaking secret-fragment removal in a real browser.
10. HTTPS on API only was insufficient because the secret-handling buyer page itself could be modified over HTTP.
11. Buyer page needed cache/referrer/frame/exfiltration hardening.
12. Creator Studio readiness became stale after manual entitlement became available.

Hard distinctions:

```text
PAID URL != BUYER ENTITLEMENT
BEARER TOKEN = ACCESS AUTHORITY
CHECKOUT OPENED != PAYMENT VERIFIED
PAID HOSTED V0 + SHARED SUBSIDY = BLOCKED
SECRET-HANDLING PAGE OVER HTTP != SAFE
AVAILABLE MANUAL ACTIVATION PATH != SILENT AUTO-ACTIVATION
```

